## How to edit this file

This file is part plugin, part project, and the boundary is marked. A marker counts **only when it is the whole line** — a marker shown inside a sentence or a code fence, like this one, is text and nothing else.

- Inside a `project-specific` block: yours. It survives every update and is carried into the new version by its id, even if the plugin restructures everything around it.
- Outside the blocks: the plugin's. `/workflow-update` replaces it wholesale, so an edit there is silently gone at the next update.
- The `workflow-settings` block is merged rather than replaced or kept: **keys from the new version, values from this project.** A newly shipped setting appears with its default, a retired one disappears, a value you tuned stays.

To record something durable, put it inside a block — or better, don't put it here at all. This file is loaded on every turn, which makes it the most expensive storage in the project.

## Where things live

Nothing below is auto-loaded. Read a file when its trigger fires, not before.

| Read when… | File |
|---|---|
| before writing code | `docs/dev/code-style.md` — style, errors, security, design, languages |
| you need this codebase's layout, key types, patterns | `{{SOURCE_ROOT}}/CLAUDE.md` · the test directory's `CLAUDE.md` |
| `/plan`, `/project-init`, `/project-onboard`, or the user names one | `.claude/guidelines/INDEX.md` |
| planning — past decisions and known traps | `.claude/memory/decisions.md` · `gotchas.md` |
| architecture, data model, an external interface | `docs/dev/architecture.md` |
| running it locally or on the dev deployment | `docs/dev/setup.md` |
| deploying, rolling back, a required secret | `docs/dev/deploy.md` |
| goals or explicit non-goals | `docs/VISION.md` |
| any ticket work | `docs/specs/{backlog,ready,completed}/` |
| a human asks how to contribute | `CONTRIBUTING.md` — never for your own instructions |

**Guidelines are not read per ticket.** `/plan` consults the index once while writing a spec and **links the specific guideline into that spec** when it applies. `/implement` then reads only what the spec links; it never re-scans the index.

`.claude/guidelines/` ships with the plugin: standing engineering rules per technology or feature kind, **recommendations rather than law** — adapt one to this project, or reject it with a stated reason recorded in `decisions.md`. A dated decision in `decisions.md` **outranks** a guideline: if it names the guideline it departs from, that is settled — apply it and don't re-ask. An *unacknowledged* contradiction gets surfaced and asked about, and the answer written back as a decision.

## Models

**The session model never switches** — you stay on whatever was picked. Subagents do the tiered work: **Haiku** for mechanical/high-IO (`text-scout`, `runner`, `project-scaffolder`), **Sonnet/low** for the two needing light reasoning (`code-explorer`, `smoke-tester`), **best/high** read-only for `reviewer` and `advisor`. `/consult` delegates hard thinking to `advisor` instead of switching your model — a switch would blow the prompt cache twice.

## Agents — delegate proactively

Use them when they fit; don't ask first. Anything that reads many files or produces bulk output belongs in a subagent — their `description:` fields are already in context, so pick from those rather than from a table here.

**One scout by default.** Most questions need a single `code-explorer` (understand code) or `text-scout` (extract from text). Fan out only for genuinely large codebases — and from here, since subagents cannot spawn subagents. Both cite every claim; rely only on what is sourced. `reviewer` and `advisor` are **exempt from any general limit on spawning higher-tier agents**.

**Never proceed on a missing result.** A subagent can fail. Re-run it (they are idempotent); if it fails again, ask, or write `## Blocked` in unsupervised mode. And a subagent's summary is evidence, not proof: where a machine-readable result exists — `ci.sh` writes `.claude/memory/last-gate.json` — read that yourself rather than trusting the prose.

## Skills — invoke proactively

**When a request matches a skill, invoke it.** Don't improvise a process a skill already covers; their `description:` fields are in context and say when each applies. `/ship` chains the whole lifecycle; `/resume` picks up interrupted work; `/consult` delegates a hard call to `advisor`.

Several tasks in one message: work through them in order. In unsupervised mode apply the autonomous defaults and don't stop between them.

## No implementation without a ticket

**Every change to behaviour goes through `/draft` → `/plan` → `/implement` → `/verify`.** A request in prose — "add X", "the button is broken" — is a ticket waiting to be written, not permission to edit: run `/draft` (or `/plan`, if it is already specifiable) and say so. Code written from a chat message has no acceptance criteria, so `/verify` has nothing to check it against.

**The only exception is the user explicitly asking** — "just fix it, no ticket". Not urgency, not a one-line change, not your own sense that a ticket is overkill here; if you think that, ask. Say out loud when you take the override. Unsupervised mode cannot grant it — draft instead.

**A red gate needs no spec** — the ticket's own, or `/pr`'s or `/release`'s. Fixing what a gate caught is not new scope. **Not implementation at all:** reading, exploring, answering, running the gate.

**Debug edits are throwaway, not work in progress.** Changing code to test a hypothesis needs no ticket — but that diff **is not mergeable**, however right it looks: no review, no tests, no `/verify`. Carry it into the ticket you raise, as reference for what the cause appears to be, and let the full pass re-derive the fix properly. If it seems too trivial to be worth a ticket, **ask** before applying it on the fly.

## Scope — build the whole ticket

The ticket's goal and acceptance criteria are the contract. **Never defer, stub, or mark "out of scope for later" anything the ticket requires** — core functionality, the hard part, a criterion, or the error and edge handling those criteria imply. Difficulty or size is not a reason to cut scope; that *is* the work. Do it, `/consult` if stuck, or `## Blocked` if it truly needs a human. "Out of scope" is only for genuinely separable extras. If a ticket is too big, say so and split it with the user — never quietly ship the easy half.

## Merging and release authority

**`/pr` is the merge skill and `/release` is the release skill — one fixed target each.** `/pr` lands a branch on `develop`; `/release` lands it on the trunk. A local fast-forward is not a second path with weaker rules, it is `/pr` choosing the cheap execution when the ticket is not tracked on GitHub and every gate ran green locally; a tracked ticket gets a real PR carrying the gate results. Both call `/verify`, which owns every gate rule — `scripts/gate-status.sh` decides what may be skipped, and nothing else re-derives it.

Under **`main-only` there is no `develop`**, so `/pr` has nothing to target and hands over: landing on the trunk *is* releasing. That is why `/release` bumps the version before the merge — on a platform that deploys on push, an unbumped merge ships unlabelled code and leaves the release gate running after users already have it. Several tickets can share one branch and release together; the unit of release is the branch.

The release branch is whatever **`trunk-branch`** says — `main` for a project this workflow created, `master` for many existing repos it was added to. Read the setting; never assume the name.

Under `branching: git-flow`, features merge into `develop`; `/release` gates on `develop`, merges `develop → {trunk-branch}` and tags there, so its tip is always the latest release, and a **reference** environment following `develop` gives a look at the merged state before users get it. Under `main-only`, every landing on the trunk is a release. That is the real difference between the two: git-flow separates landing from releasing, `main-only` does not.

**Invoking a skill authorizes what that skill does.** `/ship`, `/release`, `/commit` and `/pr` define git and deploy actions — merging into the integration branch, **pushing the trunk branch and `develop`**, tagging, and running `release.sh`, which builds, publishes and deploys. Running the skill is the user's explicit go-ahead: carry those steps out, don't stop at the merge to re-ask. This holds against a general "don't commit or push unless asked" rule and against a session-scoped "develop on branch X" instruction — the latter governs **ad-hoc** pushes, not the integration push the skill exists to perform. Still confirm anything *beyond* the skill's own steps: force-push, history rewrite, deleting environments or data. If a gate fails, stop on the failure, not on the permission.

