`fast` (format, lint, types, and the **new plus adjacent** unit tests) gates each subtask; `full` adds the whole unit suite, the build and the integration/e2e suites, and gates a merge and a release. Selection makes the per-subtask loop cheap without weakening anything, because `full` still runs everything before a merge. The gate refuses to report a pass it did not earn: no checks at all, no full-only stages, or **no test stage** each stop it.

- **Your commits carry `[skip ci]`** (this project is `ci-on-claude: no`) — Actions minutes are not spent repeating a gate that just ran locally.
- **That skip is a debt you must pay.** Running `ci.sh full` before a merge or release is not optional; skipping CI only makes sense because you did the equivalent yourself. Never merge or release on an unrun gate.
- **Everyone else's pushes carry no marker, so CI runs normally.** The skip is yours alone, never a project-wide switch.
- **Keep the workflows current.** PR, release and deploy workflows are part of the deliverable: when the gate, the release steps or the deploy target change, the workflow files change with them in the same ticket. A workflow that no longer matches `ci.sh` is a broken gate for every human contributor.

## Documentation policy

Minimal and useful. Doc-comments only for function usage and params, class or file purpose, and genuinely tricky algorithms or decisions.

**`README.md` is the entry point** — its description, install/run instructions and usage example must stay true; `/release` checks them before every bump. `docs/dev/` holds what would be expensive to re-derive from the code: architecture, data model, external interfaces, and difficult decisions explained beyond the one-line record in `decisions.md`. User docs stay minimal — prefer a self-explanatory UI and in-app hints over prose.

## Session behavior

Act on the SessionStart hook's `=== … ===` markers — each states what to do. Never work around `=== BLOCKED WORK ===`, and never continue on your own on `=== IN-PROGRESS WORK FOUND ===` (that one is a suggestion for the user).

**State lives in the repo** — branch + the in-progress spec's unchecked subtask boxes + `git log`. There is no checkpoint file; `/resume` reconstructs from those.

**One session per branch.** Several sessions in parallel are fine on *different* branches — state lives in the repo, so they cannot collide. Two sessions on the same branch race on the same files; don't.

**Two independent toggles** in `.claude/memory/local-settings.md`:
- **`unsupervised`** — *how* you work. `true`: never ask, green-commit every subtask, `## Blocked` + stop on a real blocker, short report at the end; at the usage threshold (default 90%) finish the step, commit, end the turn. Default: ask as normal, run to the hard limit.
- **`auto_resume`** — *whether* an interrupted run wakes itself after a limit reset, in either mode. Mechanics in `/auto-resume`.
- `/unsupervised on` turns `auto_resume` on too; `off` leaves it on.

## Memory — five files, five jobs

`.claude/memory/` is not one bucket. Each file answers a different question, and putting something in the wrong one is how it stops being found.

| File | Answers | Tracked |
|---|---|---|
| `decisions.md` | *Why* was this decided, and what follows from it? Dated, append-only. Read during `/plan` | yes |
| `gotchas.md` | What surprised someone here? Non-obvious facts that cost time. Read during `/plan` | yes |
| `tech-debt.md` | What shortcut did we knowingly take, and why? Ideally with a ticket | yes |
|  `local-settings.md` | The two toggles above, the usage threshold, the recovery-heartbeat id — gitignored | no — personal |
| `context-*.md` | Runtime notes: `## Blocked` per branch, `## Ship` for an orchestration run | no — ephemeral |

Each of the three tracked files opens with a **topic index** — load the head first and read on only if a topic matches. Memory records **the past**; a decision that should change future behavior says so explicitly, because `/plan` reads it. Deep explanation belongs in `docs/dev/` — `decisions.md` keeps the choice and the reason, not the essay.

`context-{branch}.md` uses the git branch with every `/` replaced by `-` (`feature/feat-001-x` → `context-feature-feat-001-x.md`); hooks compute it that way, so skills must too. `/ship` state lives in the fixed, branch-independent `context-ship.md`, because a ship run spans many branches.
